\f1\fs22 Sarah woke to an empty room. Voices drifted up from downstairs\'97Kathy\'92s bright chatter, Hayley\'92s lower tones, and beneath them, Eloise\'92s measured cadence. The smell of bacon rose through the apartment like an olfactory summons.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Sarah pulled on yesterday\'92s clothes\'97wrinkled, slightly rank, but serviceable. The waistband of her trousers dug in slightly where it hadn\'92t before. Three months pregnant and already her body was filing paperwork for major renovations. She could feel something different in her breasts\'97a subtle fullness, a tenderness when she moved. Nothing dramatic. Just her body quietly informing her that new management had taken over.\
She made her way downstairs, one hand on the railing. Her usual morning energy hadn\'92t returned yet\'97she felt like she was moving through slightly thicker air than everyone else.\
\'93Well, look who\'92s vertical,\'94 Kathy said as Sarah eased into the kitchen. She was at the stove, wielding a spatula over a frying pan that was losing a pitched battle with about a dozen rashers of bacon. Grease popped and spattered. Kathy didn\'92t flinch. \'93Coffee or something more maternal?\'94\
\'93Juice,\'94 Sarah said. Her usual morning coffee craving had been replaced by something that felt like a polite but firm suggestion that coffee was no longer stomach-welcome.\
\'93Smart woman.\'94 Kathy gestured with the spatula toward the far side of the kitchen. \'93Hayley\'92s wrestling the espresso weapon. Eloise is doing something architectural with fruit.\'94\
Hayley stood at a gleaming chrome espresso machine that looked less like a kitchen appliance and more like ordnance for mounting on the deck of a destroyer. \'93There are seventeen settings,\'94 she muttered. \'93Seventeen. For coffee. This is obscene.\'94\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The Arabica Armaments Model Seven was the Ferrari of espresso machines, if Ferrari made things designed to humiliate their operators. Having exhausted the traditional espresso machine market where features like \'93makes coffee\'94 and \'93doesn\'92t explode\'94 were considered adequate, Arabica Armaments Corporation (a subsidiary of Shakti Enterprises) pivoted to serving customers for whom a simple espresso represented an unacceptable lack of military-grade precision.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The Model Seven had forty-three independently adjustable parameters, though twenty-six were locked behind an administrative interface that required biometric authentication and, according to rumour, a background check. They governed such critical functions as extraction pressure (variable by half-atmosphere increments), water temperature (adjustable to 0.1\'b0C), and something the administrator\'92s manual called \'93aggressive foam stabilisation protocols\'94 which sounded more suited to chemical warfare than breakfast beverages. The grinder was German, the pump Italian, and the logic board Israeli military surplus, creating a perfect storm of over-engineering that could produce a flawless ristretto while simultaneously judging you for your inadequate skills as a barista.\
The machine\'92s AI\'97which had been trained on the personas of Michelin-starred chefs, Italian caf\'e9 owners, and at least one PAPER interrogator\'97could discern the origin of coffee bean by scent alone, a capability it shared with exactly three humans worldwide, all of whom worked for Interpol\'92s drug enforcement division and none of whom had been consulted about their skillset being miniaturised into a countertop appliance with more processing power than most cars.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Hayley located the 
\fs18 AUTO
\fs22  setting and was pressing it with the cautious hope of someone disarming a landmine.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 At the island bench, Eloise was indeed doing something architectural\'97arranging sliced fruit on a plate, as well as filling a large blender jug with the precision of someone building a small civilisation. Mango, strawberries, something purple Sarah didn\'92t recognise. \
\'93Oh, you\'92re awake, good,\'94 Eloise said without looking up. \'93Fruit juice?\'94\
\'93Yes, please.\'94\
\'93Excellent. You\'92ll need the vitamins.\'94 Eloise added a handful of something green and leafy that Sarah suspected was kale or some other virtuous vegetable. \'93And the fibre. Trust me on this.\'94\
Sarah decided not to ask what Eloise knew about pregnancy digestive dynamics.\
Within fifteen minutes\'97through some miracle of coordinated chaos\'97they\'92d assembled an actual breakfast. The bacon was crisped to within an inch of its life, Hayley successfully extracted coffee from the chrome monster without it exploding, Eloise blended juice that was an alarming shade of purple-green, and someone located pancakes in a warming drawer Sarah hadn\'92t known existed. They found maple syrup in the walk-in stasis room that was large enough to stock a small supermarket.\
Chainsaw appeared in the kitchen doorway and produced a yowl that suggested he\'92d been personally victimised by everyone in the room. He bypassed Hayley entirely\'97the woman who fed him, housed him and paid his vet bills\'97walked directly to Eloise, and sat at her feet with the devotional intensity of a convert who\'92d found their calling. \'93You\'92ve got to be taxing kidding me.\'94 Hayley said.\
Eloise disappeared into the stasis fridge and returned with a crystal dish of sashimi-grade tuna. She placed it on the floor in front of Chainsaw, who attacked it with the enthusiasm of someone who\'92d discovered fine dining was a thing.\
\'93Bluefin?\'94 Hayley asked flatly.\
\'93Sustainable fisheries,\'94 Eloise confirmed.\
\'93How long has that been in there?\'94\
\'93Something I instructed my caterers to source, the day you arrived,\'94 Eloise answered. \'93I\'92m surprised you never found it.\'94\
\'93I found it,\'94 Kathy confessed, \'93but I had no idea it was meant for Chainsaw.\'94\
\'93Did I not say I would see to all your needs?\'94 Eloise replied with a certain degree of superiority. She leant down and stroked Chainsaw from head to tail. \'93Who\'92s a good pussy cat? Who\'92s a good Chainsaw?\'94\
\'93You\'92re a sellout,\'94 Hayley told her cat. \'93He\'92s already forgotten who cleans his litter box.\'94\
Chainsaw finished his tuna, licked the crystal dish clean, and followed Eloise as she carried her tea toward the patio. When she stepped outside and closed the door behind her, he pressed himself against the glass with the wounded dignity of someone whose civil rights had been violated.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 They carried everything out to a sturdy timber table on the patio. The morning air was sharp and clean in a way that made Sarah\'92s lungs feel like she\'92d been living in a karaoke bar for thirty years.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Sarah took only juice; her stomach was filing formal complaints about the bacon smell despite her brain\'92s enthusiastic support. The nausea was subtle but persistent, like having a very polite heckler living in her abdomen. Hayley and Kathy ate like people who\'92d just remembered food existed, while Eloise picked at a single piece of bacon and sipped weak tea from a cup that probably cost more than the stock at a Wedgwood retailer.\
\'93This air.\'94 Kathy leaned back in her chair. \'93I feel like I can breathe for the first time in my life.\'94\
\'93That\'92s because you can,\'94 Eloise said. \'93No Layer. Not this side of Springwood.\'94\
\'93How?\'94 Hayley asked, immediately suspicious.\
\'93Geography. Height above sea level.\'94\
They sat quietly for a moment, listening to magpies warble. Sarah felt something unknot in her chest\'97some tension she hadn\'92t realised she\'92d been carrying. Then her stomach did a small, queasy flip, and she remembered exactly what she was carrying.\
Hayley set down her coffee cup with the deliberate care of someone who\'92d reached a decision. \'93How long do you plan on keeping us here?\'94\
\'93I have no such plans, but you may consider staying until I can be sure PAPER is back on its chain,\'94 Eloise said.\
\'93And who will put your son back on his chain?\'94 Hayley persisted, unintimidated. \'93That\'92s my problem, I suppose?\'94\
\'93Alexander will always be my problem,\'94 Eloise said, and cast a sympathetic glance at Sarah that made her want to throw something.\
\'93Could I sneak back to my apartment and collect a few things?\'94 Kathy asked.\
\'93Make lists of everything you want. I\'92ll make sure you have it by sundown,\'94 Eloise said with the casual confidence of someone who could make problems dissolve at the snap of her purse clasp.\
\'93Yeah, it\'92s cold out here,\'94 Sarah said, wrapping her arms around herself. The autumn was cooling towards winter, and the morning sun hadn\'92t quite warmed the chill yet. Sarah felt it more acutely than usual\'97her body temperature seemed to be running slightly warmer and slightly more sensitive to change. \'93I have nothing to wear.\'94\
\'93There are coats and sensible shoes in the hall closet. You can make do today,\'94 Eloise said.\
\'93It\'92s just,\'94 Kathy said, working her way up to something, \'93I have certain medicinal requirements\'85\'94\
\'93Yes. Your patches,\'94 Eloise said, meeting just another negotiation. \'93Whatever you need, I can provide at the highest quality.\'94\
\'93Oh, I dunno. I cook a lot of my own compounds.\'94\
Hayley stiffened. \'93Ah, police officer here!\'94 She pointed to herself.\
\'93Oh, of course,\'94 Kathy said, then to Eloise: \'93I need enough for all three of us.\'94\
Hayley made a sound like the espresso machine might have poisoned her after all. Eloise smiled with genuine amusement. \'93It seems you want to party with the only clean police officer in all of Ulyssia.\'94\
Kathy leaned toward Hayley. \'93It\'92s okay. I\'92m sure you\'92re fun even without chemical assistance.\'94\
Hayley\'92s face went through several expressions in rapid succession\'97surprise, embarrassment, consideration, then something that might have been pleased. She settled on scowling at her coffee.\
\'93I\'92m sure she would be,\'94 Eloise agreed. \'93Make those lists. Clothes, jewellery, perfumes, gymnasium equipment, favourite teddy bears\'85\'94\
\'93Food?\'94 Kathy asked.\
\'93The stasis closet is abundantly stocked, but I\'92ll send someone down from catering if you have a particular want. You can even have one of my chefs, if you like.\'94\
Hayley brightened visibly. \'93Yes, please. All I can cook is coffee.\'94 Then her face clouded. \'93I left all my computer gear at home. I still have much to investigate.\'94\
\'93No,\'94 Eloise said. \'93I\'92ll provide fresh hardware. Everything you need.\'94\
The way she said it gave Hayley the distinct impression the hardware would come preloaded with her police apps, authentication dongles and enough covert surveillance capability to catalogue the nostril hairs of every citizen in the country. She drained the last drops of her tea. \'93Sarah. Walk with me, will you?\'94\
\'93Ah, I could really use a shower\'85\'94\
\'93I\'92m sure you could imagine the value I place on my own time, so if I\'92m willing to spend some of it with you, I would ask if you could be as gracious with yours. Besides, I promise, we\'92ll stay outdoors.\'94\
Sarah stood. The weariness that had been her constant companion for the past three months made the prospect of a walk feel optimistic, but she wasn\'92t about to admit that to Eloise.\
Kathy caught her eye, raised an eyebrow in question. Sarah gave a small nod\'97I\'92m fine. Hayley looked ready to stand up and follow, but Kathy\'92s hand on her arm kept her seated.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Eloise led Sarah from the townhouse\'92s back yard into a large pentagonal garden. At its centre sat an abstract fountain that looked like someone had frozen a splash of water mid-flight and cast it in bronze. The morning sun caught the water spray, making tiny rainbows dance.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \'93Are any of the other houses occupied?\'94 Sarah asked, gesturing at the four other residences arranged around the garden\'92s perimeter\'97each one larger than most Airborne penthouses, set at angles that gave them privacy from each other.\
\'93Not at present.\'94 Eloise took Sarah through one of the other yards, out across a roundabout, and into the native bushland. Then Sarah\'92s feet found a track\'97packed earth, barely wider than a footpath. It wound through stands of eucalypts, some with papery bark peeling in long strips.\
Within minutes they\'92d left all buildings behind. The only sounds were the crackle of dry leaves and twigs and the scrunch of grainy soil underfoot. From the trees, noisy miners called shrilly, and occasionally rainbow lorikeets squawked. A bush turkey ran across the path in front of Sarah, and startled her.\
The path curved, climbed slightly, then opened onto bare sandstone. Sarah\'92s breath came easier here; clear of the heady smell of eucalyptus, the air tasted clean in a way she\'92d forgotten was possible. Despite her fatigue, she felt her lungs expanding.\
Sarah stopped.\
The ground ended.\
Not gradually\'97it just ended. As if someone had taken a cleaver to the plateau and carved away everything beyond this point. Steel railings ran along the edge, looking impossibly thin against the vast emptiness beyond them.\
Eloise walked forward with the confidence of someone who\'92d made this walk a thousand times. Sarah followed more carefully, one hand finding the railing before she trusted her feet. The fatigue in her legs made her more cautious than usual\'97she didn\'92t trust her usual reflexes as much as she had a month ago.\
They reached a narrow metal platform that jutted out from the cliff edge. On three sides: nothing. Just air and the valley below and the sky above. Sarah\'92s stomach grumbled about the complex mathematics of gravity and terminal velocity. The tides of her nausea, diminished by the walk, lifted with the vertigo brought on by the expansive slash of open space.\
The valley floor was so far down it looked hypothetical. Treetops were loops of a single rolling green rug. A sulphur-crested cockatoo wheeled below them, its wings catching the light. The sandstone cliffs across the valley were layered in shades of gold and ochre and rust, each stratum a million years of patient geology and erosion. Beyond them, more ridges, more valleys, rolling away to a horizon that seemed impossibly distant.\
\'93Let\'92s have a seat,\'94 Eloise said.\
Sarah tore her eyes from the void. There was a wooden bench bolted to the platform, facing south across the valley. The narrow walkway to reach it had waist-high railings on both sides; Sarah could lean over them and see straight down the drops on either side.\
They sat. Eloise settled in the middle with the ease of someone sitting in her own lounge room. Sarah took the near end, leaving half a metre between them, one hand still on the armrest like an anchor. She pressed her other hand to her abdomen\'97not because she could feel anything there except the persistent low-level nausea, but because her body kept insisting something important was happening in that general area and she should pay attention.\
\'93Quite a sight, isn\'92t it?\'94 Eloise said quietly.\
From the bench, Sarah was thankful she could no longer look down, only across the valley floor hundreds of metres below. The trees looked like moss, the rock formations like petrified marble cakes broken apart by titanic hands. A secluded creek caught the light, and traced a silver thread for a moment through the green.\
Her hand stayed pressed against her abdomen. Nothing moved there\'97nothing she could feel, anyway. But she knew something was there. A cluster of cells, dividing and organising themselves into something that would eventually be a person. In about six months, if her body cooperated, if Eloise\'92s plans worked, if any of this worked at all.\
Her eyes stung.\
Her throat felt tight.\
Her vision blurred. She blinked hard.\
Eloise handed her a handkerchief without looking.\
\'93I\'92m sorry,\'94 Sarah said. \'93I don\'92t know why lately, I feel so\'85\'94 She wiped the corners of her eyes and dabbed around her nostrils.\
\'93Oh, just blow your damn nose,\'94 Eloise chided. \'93We have a laundry on site, you know.\'94\
Sarah sputtered a laugh, bugled into the handkerchief.\
\'93Elegant,\'94 Eloise said dryly.\
Sarah laughed again, longer and more easily, embarrassed. Some of the pressure eased from her chest.\
Eloise nodded toward the southwest. \'93You see that formation in the distance? Just back from the escarpment? Those three distinct rock formations?\'94\
Sarah squinted against the stark morning sun reflecting off the ridges. \'93I think I see them, yes.\'94 She nodded.\
\'93They\'92re called the Three Sisters,\'94 Eloise said. \'93You see them better from the western side. But even from here\'85\'94 She paused. \'93They\'92ve stood there for hundreds of millions of years. The three of them, supporting each other. I suspect if one ever cracked and fell, the others would follow.\'94\
Sarah waited. Eloise was building to something.\
Eloise let the silence hold. \'93You\'92ll need sisters of your own.\'94\
\'93Kathy?\'94\
Eloise kept her gaze on the distant peaks. \'93I think you need one on either side.\'94\
Sarah considered. \'93Hayley.\'94\
Eloise nodded, still looking at the Three Sisters. A breeze tousled her immaculate hairstyle; she restored it with a casual wave of her hand. \'93Detective Superintendent Hayley Lanning is a police officer who\'92s not crooked, who\'92s fearless, thorough, resourceful\'85 and who\'92s absolutely smitten with Kathy.\'94\
\'93What?\'94\
\'93Given the right circumstances and motivation, she\'92ll be intensely loyal to both of you.\'94\
\'93Really.\'94\
\'93Indeed. I\'92m thinking of offering her a job. Kathy too.\'94\
\'93And what would you have them do?\'94\
\'93Anything you require.\'94\
\'93You\'92re not at all what I expected from a warlord.\'94\
Eloise let the dysphemism pass. \'93That\'92s why I\'92m good at it.\'94\
Sarah considered that. \'93Are you offering me a job too?\'94\
\'93My darling, capable Sarah. You can have a seat on the board, if you wish.\'94\
\'93And jobs for Kathy and Hayley.\'94\
\'93I don\'92t know if you\'92ve noticed, but Kathy has quite an advanced understanding of certain plant extracts and pharmaceutical compounds. Very useful. And Hayley\'92s\'85 integrity\'85 might seem surplus to requirements for a position in my organisation, but I value it.\'94\
Sarah frowned. \'93You killed one of your bodyguards\'97\'94\
\'93Contractor.\'94\
\'93\'97right before me, just to scare me. Where\'92s the integrity in that?\'94\
\'93Believe me, Sarah, hard decisions require more integrity, especially those that are harsh on localised parts of the whole system.\'94\
\'93How many of your employees die in your service?\'94\
Eloise pursed her lips. \'93Year-on-year we\'92re down 3.4%,\'94 she said crisply. \'93People and Culture is particularly pleased with that figure. They prefer to fire them than expire them. The only thing they hate more than employee mortality is voluntary turnover.\'94\
\'93They prefer deaths over resignations\'85\'94 Sarah processed.\
\'93The dead don\'92t break non-compete clauses.\'94\
Sarah looked out at the valley. \'93Why? Why me?\'94\
\'93Because you are the mother of my one and only heir.\'94\
\'93Little Clinton is not your grandchild.\'94\
\'93I\'92m adopting him.\'94\
\'93Alexander can give you grandchildren.\'94\
Eloise tossed her head. \'93Holy accountants preserve us!\'94 Her lips went momentarily thin while she composed herself. \'93I have few expectations of the world after I\'92ve gone to the great second-hand arms market in the sky, but I am determined to ensure\'97and believe me, I say this with the greatest love for my Alexander\'97no issue of my son shall inherit any\'85\'94 She huffed. \'93Anything.\'94\
Sarah sat quiet for a moment, her hand still pressed to her abdomen where nothing visible was happening but where everything was changing. \'93So I\'92m just an incubator to you.\'94\
Eloise stabbed a look of scorn at Sarah. \'93If you were merely that, I\'92d put you in a coma until your baby was born, then discard you.\'94\
Those words froze Sarah\'92s blood. She knew Eloise was capable of such an atrocity, had probably committed similar evil deeds countless times. \'93Is that your backup plan?\'94\
Eloise let her head swing in slow arcs of refusal. \'93Don\'92t wear at my patience, Sarah. Don\'92t test me. And never, ever, invite an audience with my darker angels.\'94\
Sarah watched Eloise watching the horizon, studied her jaw gently grinding emotional gristle between her molars. She knew if she didn\'92t say it now, she\'92d\'97 \'93I can\'92t marry Alexander.\'94\
\'93You can and you will. And we will all be better for it.\'94\
\'93But\'97\'94 Sarah shuddered as she groped for words. Her hand pressed harder against her belly, as if that could somehow communicate to the cluster of cells inside what was being negotiated on its behalf. \'93I can\'92t be a wife. I never wanted children.\'94\
Eloise met Sarah\'92s gaze for the first time since they\'92d left the townhouse, and saw fearful dread in her eyes. \'93So be it.\'94\
A small cloud drifted across the edge of the valley. Eloise scrutinised it for a moment, assessing it wasn\'92t an errant puff of Layer straying across from the eastern ranges. \'93Alexander doesn\'92t want a wife,\'94 she continued. \'93He wants the appearance of a wife. He wants another trophy\'97a shiny trophy he can worship and put on a pedestal for a while, and then toss in a closet.\'94\
\'93Well, thanks for selling it. I feel much better.\'94\
\'93The point is, he\'92ll move onto the next thing. Ironically, the best way to be free of him is to give him what he wants, and wait till he tires of it.\'94\
\'93And how long will that take?\'94\
\'93Let me manage that.\'94\
\'93And in the meantime? I can\'92t\'85 live\'85 with him.\'94 Sarah shuddered again.\
In her peripheral vision, Eloise saw it. She\'92d honed the skill of observing people and things without appearing to do so. \'93I\'92ll advise him you should stay here until after the birth. That should give us time. And he\'92s already excited about my international syndication of Candid Criminal. I could have him travelling the globe until well after Christmas\'97send him to Africa or Europe or\'85 Tajikistan or, I don\'92t know\'85 Idaho.\'94\
\'93You just keep giving him bigger trophies. Sooner or later\'97\'94\
\'93I can outrun my son.\'94\
\'93So a woman\'92s job is still to placate a man\'97even as his demands become more unreasonable?\'94\
\'93Don\'92t be ridiculous, Sarah. That\'92s a mother\'92s job.\'94\
\'93A mother of sons.\'94\
Eloise stood and stared Sarah down, her eyes fierce and her lips thin as blades. \'93Look Sarah, either work with me or throw yourself off this lookout right now. I\'92m tired of this.\'94 She took her thunderstorm of anger into the bush, back toward the compound.\
For many minutes after, Sarah sat on the bench and felt the threat of lightning in the air. She wasn\'92t entirely sure the thunderstorm had been real. Her hand stayed pressed to her abdomen\'97still nothing she could feel, but her body insisted on making the gesture anyway, as if practicing for a future when there would be something to hold.\
Sarah looked out at the Three Sisters in the distance, that had stood together for longer than humans had words for time.\
She wondered if she\'92d still be standing in six months, or if the ground would rise up to meet her the way it eventually met everything that flew too high for too long.
